**CRITIQUE**

- **The close explains rung 4 twice.** "Fences need leaning on / It's what the trade was for" already carries the retirement. The mythic speech then re-derives it — "The lineage was never meant to keep teaching forever. It was meant to teach well enough that it stops needing the teacher" — which also pre-says the italic moral. Cut the speech at "you'll know what to tell him"; the transmission beat survives, the essay doesn't.
- **Rung 5 still pre-chews the click.** The kōan works — wrong twice, turned to watching, green dog, the right question. Then the father answers with a triple-list ("enough trained animals, enough notebooks poured into blood, enough old lessons standing around in young dogs") and two doctrinal names. After the click, two short sentences beat one paragraph.
- **Rung 4 states its revelation three ways** — "the teaching moves inside the fence," "the kennel becomes its own trainer," "no whisperer in the ring — not anymore, not for that." One formulation suffices; keep Xenophon and "Every rung before it was a means. This is the end," which is the best sentence in the draft.
- **The wax-tablet filing beat is essay voice.** "He filed the correction away the way you file the moment you learn a story you've always known has a hole in it" narrates an interiority the fable can show in four words of behavior. Also cut the opening's "Some lessons only make sense stacked on older ones" — the image already says it.
- **Small doubles throughout:** "on its back every morning" / "climbs on every morning"; "tireless" / "never tires"; "once-started is started for good" / "makes the next colt cheaper than the last"; "the large one… the large one." Each pair kept one, cut one — that's most of the ~90 words brought down.

